  "worked_solution": "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one by one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{\u2032}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,2,\\,2t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{\u2032}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{\u2032}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t+5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t+1})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{e}^{t+5},\\,\\frac{1}{3(t+1)^{3/2}}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{\u2032}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{\u2032}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,1,\\,{sec}^{2}\u2061t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the functions are differentiable on $t\\in (0,1).$\"",
